WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.344
Host: Compare the role of calcium in skeletal,
cardiac and smooth muscle.

2
00:00:05.344 --> 00:00:10.947
Guide: Skeletal and cardiac muscle use
troponin C to regulate actin access.

3
00:00:10.947 --> 00:00:20.035
Guide: Skeletal coupling primarily releases
stored calcium through voltage-sensor
coupling; cardiac muscle needs trigger calcium
entry.

4
00:00:20.035 --> 00:00:26.703
Guide: Smooth muscle uses calcium–calmodulin
and myosin light-chain kinase to regulate
myosin.

5
00:00:26.703 --> 00:00:33.407
Host: Why can normal skeletal muscle produce
tetanic force while normal cardiac working
muscle cannot?

6
00:00:33.407 --> 00:00:38.260
Guide: The cardiac refractory period overlaps
much of its contraction.

7
00:00:38.260 --> 00:00:46.378
Guide: The long refractory period limits
repeated excitation and summation into
tetanus, preserving filling between beats.
